const faq = faqPageSchema([
  { question: "What are Non-QM loans?", answer: "Non-QM loans are home financing solutions that fall outside the qualified mortgage definition established by the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau. They serve borrowers who do not meet conventional documentation requirements but still demonstrate a reasonable ability to repay, such as self-employed individuals, real estate investors, and those with complex income sources." },
  { question: "What is the difference between a DSCR loan and a Non-QM loan?", answer: "All DSCR loans are Non-QM loans, but not all Non-QM loans are DSCR loans. DSCR loans are a specific type designed for investment properties that qualify based on the property's rental income rather than the borrower's personal income." },
  { question: "Is a bank statement loan a Non-QM loan?", answer: "Yes. Bank statement loans use 12 to 24 months of bank statements instead of tax returns to verify income, which places them outside conventional qualified mortgage guidelines." },
  { question: "What property types are eligible for Non-QM loans?", answer: "Eligible property types include primary residences, investment properties, mixed-use buildings, non-warrantable condominiums, co-ops, and some properties in distressed sale situations." },
  { question: "What banks do Non-QM loans?", answer: "The Non-QM market is primarily served by non-bank mortgage lenders, credit unions, and private lenders. Major banks have largely focused on conventional loans. Specialized mortgage lenders like Cliffco Mortgage Bankers offer a broad range of Non-QM programs." },
]);
---

<Layout
  title="Non-QM Loans: The Complete Guide | Cliffco Mortgage Bankers"
  description="Everything you need to know about non-qualified mortgage loans: who they're for, how they work, types of programs, and how to qualify."
  type="article"
  schema={faq}
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Mortgage Guides", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "Non-QM Loans Guide", href: "/mortgage-guides/non-qm-loans-guide/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">Non-QM Loans: The Complete Guide</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8">Non-QM stands for non-qualified mortgage: any loan that doesn't meet the CFPB's "qualified mortgage" definition established after the 2008 housing crisis. That definition is built around specific documentation requirements, debt-to-income thresholds, and loan features, and a lot of financially strong borrowers don't fit it.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">This guide covers what Non-QM loans are, which programs exist, how lenders qualify borrowers differently, and who these loans are actually for.</p>

    <nav class="mt-10 p-6 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-lg">
      <p class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-3">Table of Contents</p>
      <ul class="space-y-1 text-sm text-[var(--color-accent)]">
        <li><a href="#what-are-non-qm-loans" class="hover:underline">What Are Non-QM Loans?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#types-of-non-qm-loans" class="hover:underline">Types of Non-QM Loans</a></li>
        <li><a href="#dscr-vs-non-qm" class="hover:underline">What Is the Difference Between a DSCR Loan and a Non-QM Loan?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bank-statement-loan" class="hover:underline">Is a Bank Statement Loan a Non-QM Loan?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#eligible-property-types" class="hover:underline">What Property Types Are Eligible for Non-QM Loans?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#requirements" class="hover:underline">Non-QM Loan Requirements</a></li>
        <li><a href="#who-should-consider" class="hover:underline">Borrowers Who May Consider a Non-QM Loan</a></li>
        <li><a href="#benefits" class="hover:underline">Benefits of Non-QM Loans</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-banks" class="hover:underline">What Banks Do Non-QM Loans?</a></li>
        <li><a href="#choosing-a-lender" class="hover:underline">Choosing a Trusted Non-QM Mortgage Lender</a></li>
        <li><a href="#conclusion" class="hover:underline">Conclusion</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="what-are-non-qm-loans" class="mt-12">What Are Non-QM Loans?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM loans don't meet the CFPB's qualified mortgage rules, but that doesn't mean the borrowers aren't qualified. It means the loan uses a different method to verify the borrower's ability to repay. Tax returns aren't the only way to document income. A debt-to-income ratio isn't the only way to evaluate risk. Non-QM programs build the case differently.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">These are not the subprime products of the mid-2000s. Today's Non-QM loans use real underwriting: bank statements, asset verification, rental income analysis, P&L statements. The difference from conventional loans is in what documentation counts, not whether documentation is required.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">At Cliffco, we work across the full range of Non-QM programs. Many of the borrowers we help have strong financial profiles. They simply earn in a way that conventional guidelines weren't built to evaluate.</p>

    <h2 id="types-of-non-qm-loans" class="mt-12">Types of Non-QM Loans</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM is a category, not a single product. Several distinct programs fall under it, each designed around a different documentation method or borrower type.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Bank Statement Loans</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Designed for self-employed borrowers and business owners. Rather than relying on tax returns (which may show lower net income due to legitimate write-offs), bank statement loans use 12 to 24 months of personal or business bank statements to verify income. This reflects the actual cash flow of an entrepreneur or independent professional more accurately than a tax return does.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">DSCR Loans (Debt Service Coverage Ratio)</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Designed for real estate investors. Instead of reviewing the borrower's personal income, DSCR loans evaluate whether the property's rental income is sufficient to cover the mortgage payment. No tax returns, no employment verification. The property either qualifies on its cash flow or it doesn't.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">P&amp;L Loans</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">For self-employed borrowers who can provide a profit and loss statement prepared by a CPA. Useful when tax returns don't reflect current business performance: for example, when a business has grown significantly in the past year but returns are based on older figures.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">VOE Loans</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Verification of Employment loans confirm income directly with an employer rather than through tax returns or pay stubs. Useful for borrowers with stable employment but compensation structures (commissions, bonuses, contract income) that don't document cleanly through traditional methods.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">1099 Loans</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">For independent contractors and freelancers. These loans use IRS Form 1099 income over 12 to 24 months as the basis for qualification. Useful for gig workers or long-term contractors with consistent earnings that don't show up as W-2 income.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Asset Depletion Loans</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">For borrowers with significant liquid assets but limited documented income: retirees, high-net-worth individuals, and others who hold wealth rather than earn traditional salary. The lender calculates a notional monthly income by dividing the borrower's eligible assets over a set period, using that figure to qualify.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Asset Based Loans</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Qualification based on liquid assets rather than income or tax returns. Investment portfolios, retirement accounts, and other liquid holdings are used to demonstrate the ability to repay. Useful for borrowers with substantial wealth but minimal taxable income.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Interest-Only</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Certain Non-QM programs offer interest-only payment periods, typically 5 to 10 years, which reduce the monthly payment during that window. After the interest-only period, the loan transitions to fully amortizing payments. A useful structure for borrowers with irregular or seasonal income who prefer lower required payments with the option to pay more when cash flow allows.</p>

    <h2 id="dscr-vs-non-qm" class="mt-12">What Is the Difference Between a DSCR Loan and a Non-QM Loan?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">All DSCR loans are Non-QM loans, but not all Non-QM loans are DSCR loans. DSCR is a specific type of Non-QM program, designed for investment properties, that qualifies based on the property's rental cash flow rather than the borrower's personal income.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The Debt Service Coverage Ratio measures rental income against the total mortgage payment. If a property generates $2,000 per month in rent and has a $1,500 PITIA payment, the DSCR is 1.33. Most lenders require a minimum DSCR of 1.0. Cliffco's minimum is also 1.0, meaning the property at least breaks even.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">What distinguishes DSCR from other Non-QM programs:</p>
    <ul class="mt-3 list-disc list-inside space-y-1">
      <li>The property qualifies, not the borrower's personal income.</li>
      <li>No personal income verification required.</li>
      <li>No employment history required.</li>
      <li>Portfolio scaling isn't capped by personal DTI.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4">DSCR loans are Non-QM because they bypass standard income verification: the property's performance is the underwriting story. For investors building rental portfolios, that distinction matters a great deal.</p>

    <h2 id="bank-statement-loan" class="mt-12">Is a Bank Statement Loan a Non-QM Loan?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Yes. Bank statement loans use 12 to 24 months of bank statements in place of tax returns to verify income, which places them outside conventional qualified mortgage guidelines.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">They're particularly useful for self-employed borrowers whose tax returns reflect lower net income due to business deductions, even when their actual cash flow is strong. Many business owners run substantial revenue through their accounts month after month but show modest income on a tax return because they write off equipment, payroll, rent, and other legitimate expenses. Bank statement loans read the deposits, not the bottom line.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">At Cliffco, we've helped a lot of borrowers qualify through bank statement programs after being turned down by lenders who only looked at tax returns. The income was always there. It just needed to be documented differently.</p>

    <h2 id="eligible-property-types" class="mt-12">What Property Types Are Eligible for Non-QM Loans?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM programs cover a wider range of property types than conventional financing. Eligible types typically include:</p>

    <h3 class="mt-6">Primary Residences</h3>
    <p class="mt-2">Single-family homes, condominiums, townhouses, and 2 to 4 unit properties where the borrower occupies one unit.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-6">Investment Properties</h3>
    <p class="mt-2">Single-family rentals, multi-unit buildings, and apartment complexes. DSCR programs are specifically designed for these, qualifying on the property's rental income rather than the borrower's personal income.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-6">Mixed-Use Properties</h3>
    <p class="mt-2">Buildings combining residential and commercial uses, which often don't qualify for conventional financing.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-6">Non-Warrantable Condominiums</h3>
    <p class="mt-2">Condos that don't meet Fannie Mae or Freddie Mac guidelines due to investor concentration, pending litigation, or other factors that block conventional financing.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-6">Co-Ops</h3>
    <p class="mt-2">Cooperative housing units, which are frequently excluded from traditional mortgage programs.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-6">Distressed or Transitional Properties</h3>
    <p class="mt-2">Properties coming out of foreclosure, short sales, or in need of rehabilitation may qualify under certain Non-QM programs.</p>

    <h2 id="requirements" class="mt-12">Non-QM Loan Requirements</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM loans are more flexible than conventional financing, but they still require real documentation. The specific requirements vary by program.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Credit Score</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Most Non-QM programs require a minimum credit score of 620 to 680, depending on the program and loan-to-value. Higher scores get better rates. Many programs can work with scores in the mid-600s when compensating factors are present, like strong reserves or a low LTV.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Down Payment</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM loans generally require 10% to 20% down, compared to the 3% to 5% available on some conventional programs. Larger down payments improve rates and make borderline files easier to approve.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Income Verification</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">This is where Non-QM differs most from conventional. Rather than requiring W-2s and tax returns, programs may use bank statements, P&L statements, 1099s, asset documentation, or (for DSCR) property rental income. The specific method depends on the loan type and how you earn.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Loan Terms</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM programs typically offer 15 to 40-year terms. Some include interest-only periods. Rate and term options vary by program and lender.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Reserves</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Most programs require 3 to 12 months of PITIA reserves held in liquid accounts after closing. The higher the LTV or the more complex the file, the more reserves typically help.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Debt-to-Income Ratio</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Conventional loans generally cap DTI at 43%. Non-QM programs may allow up to 50% or higher with compensating factors. For DSCR loans, personal DTI is not a factor at all.</p>

    <h2 id="who-should-consider" class="mt-12">Borrowers Who May Consider a Non-QM Loan</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM programs fit a specific type of borrower: financially capable, but earning or documenting income in a way that conventional guidelines weren't designed for.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Self-employed borrowers</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Business owners, freelancers, and entrepreneurs whose tax returns show lower net income due to deductions, even when their actual cash flow is strong. Bank statement and P&L programs are built for this.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Borrowers with irregular income</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Commission-based earners, seasonal workers, contractors, and those with multiple income streams. The income is real and consistent. It just doesn't follow a W-2 pattern.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Real estate investors</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Investors building rental portfolios often hit DTI walls with conventional financing. DSCR loans let properties qualify on their own cash flow, keeping the investor's personal income out of the equation entirely.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Borrowers after credit events</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM programs often have shorter waiting periods after foreclosures or bankruptcies than conventional loans, sometimes as little as one to two years rather than the four to seven years conventional typically requires.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">High-net-worth borrowers with low taxable income</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Borrowers with substantial liquid assets or investment portfolios but minimal W-2 income. Asset depletion and asset-based programs qualify on holdings rather than paycheck history.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Foreign nationals</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-U.S. citizens purchasing property in America frequently can't document income through U.S. tax returns. Specialized Non-QM programs accommodate the documentation realities of foreign national buyers.</p>

    <h2 id="benefits" class="mt-12">Benefits of Non-QM Loans</h2>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Alternative income documentation</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Bank statements, P&L statements, 1099s, and asset documentation can all substitute for tax returns, depending on the program. This is the core benefit: your income doesn't have to look a specific way on paper to qualify.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Shorter waiting periods after credit events</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Some Non-QM programs can work with borrowers one to two years out of a foreclosure or bankruptcy, compared to the four to seven years conventional guidelines typically require.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Portfolio scaling for investors</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">DSCR programs let investors add properties without running into personal DTI limits. Each property qualifies on its own cash flow, not by adding to the borrower's debt burden.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Wider property coverage</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-warrantable condos, mixed-use properties, co-ops, and other property types that conventional financing won't touch are often eligible under Non-QM programs.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Higher DTI tolerance</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM programs may allow DTI ratios above the conventional 43% cap, with some programs going to 50% or higher with compensating factors.</p>

    <h2 id="what-banks" class="mt-12">What Banks Do Non-QM Loans?</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">The Non-QM market is mostly served by non-bank mortgage lenders and specialized shops, not the major retail banks. Large banks have focused their operations on conventional, FHA, and VA loans that can be sold to Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. Those products run on standardized underwriting, which doesn't accommodate the file-by-file evaluation Non-QM requires.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">At Cliffco, Non-QM is a core part of what we do, not a side product. We maintain relationships with numerous wholesale lenders and investors in the Non-QM space, which gives us access to a wide range of programs and the ability to find solutions when one program doesn't fit.</p>

    <h2 id="choosing-a-lender" class="mt-12">Choosing a Trusted Non-QM Mortgage Lender</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Lender choice matters more with Non-QM than with conventional financing. The programs are more varied, the underwriting is more judgment-based, and the range of available products differs significantly from lender to lender.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Experience in the Non-QM space</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">A lender who does occasional Non-QM business will see fewer programs and have less experience structuring complex files than one where it's a primary focus. Ask specifically about volume and which programs they have direct access to.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Program breadth</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">The right Non-QM program for your situation may not be the first one offered. A lender with access to multiple investors and program types has more options to work with when the initial fit isn't quite right.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Clear communication on terms</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM loans carry higher rates than conventional. A trustworthy lender explains why, what's driving the rate, and what options exist to improve terms: more down payment, more reserves, a different program. If a lender isn't transparent about the tradeoffs, that's worth noticing.</p>

    <h3 class="mt-8">Willingness to look at the full picture</h3>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM underwriting is more holistic than conventional. A good Non-QM lender reviews the full file before telling you what won't work. Compensating factors (strong reserves, low LTV, a high-performing property) can make a meaningful difference in what programs are available to you.</p>

    <h2 id="conclusion" class="mt-12">Conclusion</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Non-QM loans aren't a workaround for unqualified borrowers. They're a different underwriting approach for borrowers whose financial strength doesn't show up cleanly on a tax return or a debt-to-income calculation. The income is real. The assets are real. The qualification method just has to match how the borrower actually earns.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">At Cliffco, we've spent 35+ years helping borrowers that other lenders turned away. Non-QM is a large part of how we do that. If you're not sure whether a Non-QM program applies to your situation, talk to one of our loan officers. We'll tell you honestly what's available and what isn't.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 p-6 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-lg flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
      <div>
        <p class="font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Think a Non-QM loan might be right for you?</p>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Talk to a Cliffco Non-QM specialist and we'll match you with the right program.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary shrink-0">Get started</a>
    </div>

  </article>
</Layout>
